      "source_example_id": "e-14225",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving Absolute Value Equations With Negative Variable Coefficients",
      "kp_id": "e-14225"
    },
    "q-154647": {
      "dom_id": "step-q154647",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "If $|4-y|=5,$ then $y=$",
      "worked_solution": "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $|4-y|=4-y,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|4-y| & =5 \\\\ 4-y & =5 \\\\ -y & =1 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|4-y|=-(4-y),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|4-y| & =5 \\\\ -(4-y) & =5 \\\\ -4+y & =5 \\\\ y & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=9.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
