    "q-154697": {
      "dom_id": "step-q154697",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "If $|2-\\frac{3}{4}x|=2,$ then $x=$",
      "worked_solution": "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $|2-\\frac{3}{4}x|=2-\\frac{3}{4}x,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2-\\frac{3}{4}x| & =2 \\\\ 2-\\frac{3}{4}x & =2 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4}x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $-\\frac{4}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4}x & =0 \\\\ (-\\frac{4}{3})\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{4}x) & =(-\\frac{4}{3})\\cdot (0) \\\\ (\\cancel{-\\frac{4}{3}})\\cdot (\\cancel{-\\frac{3}{4}}x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|2-\\frac{3}{4}x|=-(2-\\frac{3}{4}x),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2-\\frac{3}{4}x| & =2 \\\\ -(2-\\frac{3}{4}x) & =2 \\\\ -2+\\frac{3}{4}x & =2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $\\frac{4}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}x & =4 \\\\ (\\frac{4}{3})\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}x) & =(\\frac{4}{3})\\cdot (4) \\\\ (\\cancel{\\frac{4}{3}})\\cdot (\\cancel{\\frac{3}{4}}x) & =\\frac{16}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{16}{3}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
